From: Chad Monroe <chad@monroe.io>
Date: Fri, 24 Jul 2026 03:31:48 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] netfilter: nf_flow_table: flush hw flows before block unbind

When nftables deletes or replaces a flowtable, it unbinds the flow
block callbacks before the table is freed, so the destroys issued
from nf_flow_table_free() never reach the drivers and the driver flow
entries leak. Leaked entries keep forwarding for dead conntracks and
block offload of any later flow that reuses the same cookie address.
On OpenWrt this happens on every boot and every wifi restart, both of
which replace the flowtable.

Flush the hw offload state on FLOW_BLOCK_UNBIND, while the callbacks
are still bound: settle in-flight work, tear down all flows and push
the destroys through the drivers. The gc pass runs via the
flowtable's own gc worker because a direct nf_flow_table_gc_run()
call can race the periodic worker and double-free flows.

Signed-off-by: Chad Monroe <chad@monroe.io>
---
 include/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table.h |    1 +
 net/netfilter/nf_flow_table_core.c    |   18 ++++++++++++++++++
 net/netfilter/nf_flow_table_offload.c |    6 ++++++
 3 files changed, 25 insertions(+)

--- a/include/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table.h
+++ b/include/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table.h
@@ -313,6 +313,7 @@ void nf_flow_table_gc_cleanup(struct nf_
 void nf_flow_table_cleanup(struct net_device *dev);
 
 int nf_flow_table_init(struct nf_flowtable *flow_table);
+void nf_flow_table_flush_hw(struct nf_flowtable *flow_table);
 void nf_flow_table_free(struct nf_flowtable *flow_table);
 
 void flow_offload_teardown(struct flow_offload *flow);
--- a/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table_core.c
+++ b/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table_core.c
@@ -948,6 +948,24 @@ static struct notifier_block flow_offloa
 };
 #endif
 
+/* nftables unbinds a flowtable's block callbacks before the table is
+ * freed, so destroys issued from nf_flow_table_free() no longer reach
+ * the drivers and their flow entries leak. Flush hw state here while
+ * the blocks are still bound. The gc pass runs via the gc worker
+ * since a direct walk could race the periodic worker and double-free
+ * flows; it is re-kicked first so an already-running pass that missed
+ * the teardowns is not the one we wait for.
+ */
+void nf_flow_table_flush_hw(struct nf_flowtable *flow_table)
+{
+	nf_flow_table_offload_flush(flow_table);
+	nf_flow_table_iterate(flow_table, nf_flow_table_do_cleanup, NULL);
+	mod_delayed_work(system_power_efficient_wq, &flow_table->gc_work, 0);
+	flush_delayed_work(&flow_table->gc_work);
+	nf_flow_table_offload_flush(flow_table);
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(nf_flow_table_flush_hw);
+
 void nf_flow_table_free(struct nf_flowtable *flow_table)
 {
 	mutex_lock(&flowtable_lock);
--- a/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table_offload.c
+++ b/net/netfilter/nf_flow_table_offload.c
@@ -1271,6 +1271,12 @@ int nf_flow_table_offload_setup(struct n
 	if (!nf_flowtable_hw_offload(flowtable))
 		return nf_flow_offload_xdp_setup(flowtable, dev, cmd);
 
+	/* Flush hw flows while this device's callback is still bound so
+	 * their destroys reach the driver.
+	 */
+	if (cmd == FLOW_BLOCK_UNBIND)
+		nf_flow_table_flush_hw(flowtable);
+
 	if (dev->netdev_ops->ndo_setup_tc)
 		err = nf_flow_table_offload_cmd(&bo, flowtable, dev, cmd,
 						&extack);
